      "math_academy_id": "q-96798",
      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14228956/assets/bb4f785a62492c7ec1fe8aaecdb998bb6fba6b2a181837f49f328b9782d96548.png)\n\nWhich of the following diagrams depicts the lines representing the principal axes of the quadratic curve shown above?",
      "worked_solution": "The principal axes of a curve define a coordinate system in which the curve can be represented by its (simplest) canonical equation. \n\nOur curve is a hyperbola. This curve will have the canonical equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nif one axis passes through the vertices of the hyperbola and the other axis is the perpendicular bisector of the segment connecting the two vertices.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14228956/assets/878496864c782c63e97799b7d75598abe0bc04da1cc8b0716ea315eec99e378e.png)",
